{
  "image_name": "0c09cc5ed01c1be588e19bb16834034f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how surface coatings on photocatalysts improve reaction selectivity by promoting desired pathways while blocking harmful back reactions. On the left, a metal-core, Cr2O3-shell cocatalyst sits on a semiconductor. The drawing indicates that photogenerated electrons drive proton reduction to H2 as the forward reaction, while oxygen reduction is suppressed by the shell. The shell therefore acts as a selective barrier: protons can access the active site, but O2 is prevented from participating in the undesired reaction.\n\nOn the right, a broader example shows an oxynitride particle with a Rh–Cr oxide cocatalyst before and after adding a thin surface nanolayer. Without the extra coating, both useful reactions, hydrogen evolution and water oxidation, occur, but competing reactions such as oxygen reduction and self-decomposition of the nitride component also appear possible. After nanolayer coating, the same particle still supports the forward water-splitting chemistry, yet the coating blocks those parasitic pathways, marked with red crosses. Wavy arrows inside the particles represent photogenerated electrons and holes moving to the surface.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that carefully designed shells or nanolayers steer charge carriers toward productive chemistry and protect the photocatalyst from deactivation.",
